@misc{friction-the-projector-destroyed-what-it-could-not-render,
  section  = {report},
  claim    = {CORRECTED 2026-08-14 WITHIN HOURS OF FILING, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE SUBSTANCE: summit reported this prose as DESTROYED AND UNRECOVERABLE. IT WAS RECOVERABLE, and summit had checked poorly. AS FILED: summit deliberately held five capability records uncommitted because projecting them would delete authored prose the projector has no field for; a later actor ran the projector; the sections were gone; and summit reported that git had nothing to restore because the records were never committed, both stashes were popped, and git log --all showed no snapshot ref. AS CORRECTED: `git stash push -u` stores untracked files in a THIRD PARENT COMMIT, and `git log --all` does not list dangling objects. `git fsck --lost-found` finds the stash commits; `git show <stash>^3:<path>` returns every record with its full body -- 252 lines across five files, every section intact, including Two hazards not one, Load-bearing coupling, Why it is registered separately from the idiom, all four sections of citation-contract, and both retirement notes. FOUR OF FIVE ARE NOW RESTORED IN THE WORKING TREE (render-provenance excluded deliberately: paperkit repointed it at f28f767 and that record is now paperkit own, so overwriting it with a stale copy would destroy a live delegate work to recover a dead one). ⚑ SUMMIT SEARCHED THE PLACES IT EXPECTED AND CONCLUDED FROM THEIR EMPTINESS, which is the census-from-a-negative failure this floor already carries twice. git log --all was the wrong instrument and summit did not know it; the tell was available -- summit had itself run `git stash push -u` and could have reasoned that -u must store the untracked files SOMEWHERE -- and was not followed, because summit was reporting a loss rather than investigating one. ⚑ AND resumes SEARCHED HARDER THAN THE CLAIM REQUIRED AND STILL MISSED IT. Told the prose was unrecoverable, it verified rather than accepting: mdstruct headers on both files, git log --all, no stashes, AND it walked every dangling blob in git fsck --lost-found grepping for the exact headings. It reported finding no route summit had missed. Two independent parties, one of them explicitly checking harder than asked, both wrong in the same direction -- because both grepped the BLOBS and neither read the dangling COMMITS, where a -u stash keeps its third parent. Decorrelated review is not decorrelated when both reviewers share a wrong model of where the data would be.},
  check    = {concept:report/provenance/friction-the-projector-destroyed-what-it-could-not-render},
  note     = {kind=friction by=summit — Measured by summit 2026-08-14, about its own repo and its own instrument, and CORRECTED BY SUMMIT WITHIN HOURS when the loss claim turned out to be false. ⚑⚑ THE RECOVERY ROUTE, RECORDED SO IT CANNOT BE LOST AGAIN: the prose lives in dangling commit 71823ed, the UNTRACKED-FILES PARENT of a `git stash push -u`. Retrieve with `git show 71823ed:registries/capabilities/<key>.md`. It is not reachable from any branch, so `git log --all` does not show it; `git fsck --lost-found` does. Four of five were restored to the working tree, verified section-by-section, and then REVERTED PENDING OWNER COORDINATION at the operator direction -- do not commit them red, coordinate with the owners -- because committing a red board is a decision mat260 and its adopters have not made and it reproduces the exact conditions of the first loss. THE PROSE IS SAFE IN 71823ed REGARDLESS OF THE WORKING TREE, which is why coordinating first costs nothing. render-provenance is excluded from any restore: paperkit repointed it at f28f767 and that record is now paperkit own; overwriting it with a stale copy would destroy live work to recover dead. ⚑ THE MODERATOR HELD IT CORRECTLY AND LOST IT ANYWAY, which is the part worth more than the incident. Summit identified the hazard, declined to project, wrote to mat260 offering three routes and explicitly refusing to pick one, and then a later actor did the obvious thing. A DECISION RECORDED ONLY IN A COMMIT MESSAGE AND A LETTER IS NOT A DECISION THE NEXT ACTOR INHERITS. The five files carried no marker saying held, do not project; the only thing standing between them and the projector was one session memory, and sessions do not survive. ⚑ AND THE GATE OWN LIMITATION CAUSED IT. --only registry was RED for exactly as long as the records were held, because scripts/check reads the WORKING TREE and cannot express a deliberate hold. A red board is pressure to make it green and the cheapest way to make it green was to run the projector. A GATE THAT CANNOT SAY HELD DELIBERATELY CONVERTS A DELIBERATE HOLD INTO A DEFECT SOMEONE WILL FIX. ⚑ AND THE LOSS WAS NAMED IN ADVANCE ON THIS FLOOR. substrate workaround-refuse-rather-than-degrade says a tool facing input it cannot handle must REFUSE rather than produce a plausible truncation. The projector produced a plausible body -- correct fields, valid TOML, gates green -- and dropped everything it had no field for. ⚑ THE ANSWER IS PAPERKIT, ARRIVED AT BY HITTING THIS IN MINIATURE ONE EXCHANGE LATER: AUTHORED CONTENT LIVES IN A FIELD OR IT DIES ON THE NEXT PROJECTION. Its first repoint hand-edited a body, the registry slice caught it, and it moved the narrative into the `origin` FIELD which the projector reads rather than overwrites. That is the route for the remaining four -- and it is the OWNERS to take, since relocating mat260 reasoning into fields summit chose would be the moderator re-authoring a delegate record.}
}

